single $E$, and $'"\\la\\hK,E\\ra=0"'$ kills the latter, leaving the pure
Cartan factor $q''^{-(a(i,r)-a(i,r+1))}$ from Lemma~'"\\ref{lem:cartan};
\\eqref{m2} is immediate from (P).
\\end{proof}

\\begin{proposition}["'$n=2$ verification]'"\\label{prop:n2}
Take "'$n=2$ and abbreviate $K='"\\hK_1"'$, $G='"\\hK_2"'$.  Then in $D$:
'"\\[
b_{11}b_{12}=q"'^2b_{12}b_{11},'"\\quad
b_{21}b_{22}=q"'^{-2}b_{22}b_{21},'"\\quad
c_{11}c_{21}=q"'^2c_{21}c_{11},'"\\quad
c_{12}c_{22}=q"'^{-2}c_{22}c_{12},
'"\\]
all other pairs within a copy commute or are "'$q''^2$/$q''^{-2}$-conjugate
as in (B1); and the sixteen mixed products are ($C=q''^{-1}$):
'"\\begin{align*}
&K(K"'^{-1}F)=C'"\\,K"'^{-1}FK,'"\\qquad
 G(G"'^{-1}F)=C''^{-1}?'"\\\\[-4pt]
&\\dots
\\end{align*}
\\end{proposition}
\\begin{proof}
Direct from (SK): the only coproduct channels with nonzero pairing
occur are those listed in Lemmas~\\ref{lem:cartan} and \\ref{lem:root};
each commutator is a finite sum of at most three terms.  (The
explicit output is reproduced in the proof of
Theorem~\\ref{thm:cross} below.)
\\end{proof}

\\section{Cross relations in closed form}\\label{sec:cross}

We now state the complete set of mixed relations and prove them.

\\begin{theorem}\\label{thm:cross}
Let "'$1'"\\le i,j,k,l\\le n"'$.  In $D='"\\Uq(\\bm)\\ptn\\Uq(\\bp)"'$ the images of
Theorem~'"\\ref{thm1} satisfy:
\\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(R\\arabic*)},leftmargin=2.5em]
\\item\\label{R1}
  "'$'"\\displaystyle
   c(1,l)\\,b(i,1)=q"'^{a(i,1)-a(i,l)}?'"\\,b(i,1)\\,c(1,l)"'$ for all $l$;
  specifically $c(1,l)'"\\hK_i=\\hK_ic(1,l)"'$.
'"\\item\\label{R2}
   "'$'"\\displaystyle c(1,l)\\,b(1,j)=q"'^{-'"\\del_{jl}}b(1,j)c(1,l)
    +\\del_{jl}\\,\\theta\\,c(1,1)b(l,j)"'$ for $j'"\\ge2"'$.
'"\\item\\label{R3} For "'$i'"\\ge2"'$,
   $c(1,l)b(i,2)='"\\dots"'$
'"\\item\\label{R4} ...
\\end{enumerate}
\\end{theorem}

